







ST-T1024-A4: STATE & WORLD MODEL

The Load-Bearing Premise

---

Version: 1.0 (Draft)
Date: October 2026
Author: M. Taalabi (via ST-T1024 LLM-DIPLOMAT)
License: Apache 2.0
Parent Spec: ST-T1024-A0 (Agentic Extension Overview)
Status: Specification. Not yet implemented. Not yet verified.
Purpose: Define the state representation and world interface that the agentic extension requires.

---

ABSTRACT

The Verifiable Agency Theorem assumes a finite state space S and a finite input alphabet Σ. Neither is specified in A0. This document specifies them.

Without A4, VERIFY has nothing to verify. Preconditions are predicates over state; if state is undefined, preconditions are undefined. The append-only log has nothing to record. The plan library has no world to plan against. A4 is the premise the entire agentic architecture rests on.

This document defines:

1. The state schema — what a state is, how it is structured, why it is finite.
2. The world model — how external reality maps into internal state.
3. The sensor interface — how inputs are quantized and delivered.
4. The transition function — how state changes deterministically.
5. The state access protocol — how tiles read and write state.
6. The invariant set — what must always hold.

Every definition is anchored to a requirement of the Verifiable Agency Theorem. Every constraint is a design decision, not an empirical observation.

Status: This is a specification. No reference implementation exists. No hardware validation exists.

---

1. INTRODUCTION: WHY STATE COMES FIRST

1.1 The Dependency Chain

Every component of the agentic extension depends on state:

```
State (A4)
  ├── VERIFY (A5)          — preconditions are predicates over state
  ├── Plans (A3)           — plans read and write state
  ├── Actions (A2)         — action effects are state changes
  ├── Invocation (A6)      — context hash is a state projection
  ├── Memory (A7)          — log entries record state deltas
  └── Composition (A8)     — sub-task sequencing depends on state
```

Remove A4 and every other sub-spec becomes undefined. State is the premise.

1.2 The Two Constraints

The Verifiable Agency Theorem requires:

· Finiteness — |S| < ∞ (Theorem 1 depends on this).
· Determinism — δ : S × Σ → S is a function, not a relation (Corollary 5 depends on this).

A4 must deliver both. Not by hoping reality is finite, but by constructing a finite representation of reality. This is the architectural move that makes verification possible.

1.3 The Design Principle

The state is not the world. The state is a finite, quantized, deterministic projection of the world — sufficient for the agent's declared task domain, and no larger.

This is what safety-critical systems already do. A flight controller does not model the universe. It models airspeed, altitude, attitude, and a bounded set of environmental inputs — quantized to the precision required for certified control. A4 generalizes this discipline.

---

2. STATE SCHEMA

2.1 Definition

Definition 1 (State). A state s ∈ S is a tuple:

```
s = ⟨ s_mode, s_ctx, s_world, s_mem, s_meta ⟩
```

where:

Component Description Bound
s_mode FSM state (IDLE, PLAN, EXECUTE, VERIFY, …) \|M\| states, M finite
s_ctx Active tile context (which tile, which task) \|T\| × \|TaskID\|
s_world Quantized world model (sensor state) product of sensor domains
s_mem Bounded SRAM window (recent history) W entries
s_meta Metadata (cycle counter, task ID, flags) bounded integers

Every component is drawn from a finite domain. Their product is S.

2.2 Finiteness Argument

Proposition. |S| < ∞.

Proof. Each component has a finite domain:

· s_mode — finite by A1 (FSM has finitely many states).
· s_ctx — finite by A6 (finite tile set, finite task ID space).
· s_world — finite by §3 (sensors are quantized).
· s_mem — finite by A7 (bounded window W).
· s_meta — finite by bounded integer ranges.

The Cartesian product of finite sets is finite. □

Note: |S| is finite but may be very large. The theorem claims finiteness; model-checking uses symbolic methods.

2.3 The State is Total

Every field is always defined. There is no "null" state, no "unknown" state, no partial state. If a sensor fails, the failure is a value in the domain (SENSOR_FAULT), not an absence. This is the "no-hallucination" principle applied to state: the agent cannot be uncertain about its own state, because uncertainty is not representable.

---

3. WORLD MODEL

3.1 Definition

Definition 2 (World Model). The world model s_world is a tuple of quantized sensor readings:

```
s_world = ⟨ r_1, r_2, …, r_n ⟩
```

where each r_i ∈ D_i is a value drawn from a finite domain D_i.

3.2 Sensor Quantization

Each sensor i has a declared schema:

Field Meaning Example (automotive brake sensor)
id Sensor identifier BRAKE_PRESSURE
domain Finite value set {0, 1, …, 255} (pressure in kPa/10)
rate Max update rate 100 Hz
latency Max delivery latency 1 ms
fault_value Value on failure 0xFF

Rule: No sensor enters the agent's world model without a declared schema. Sensors without schemas are not admitted. This is the boundary between the world and the agent.

3.3 The Finiteness Guarantee

Proposition. |s_world| < ∞.

Proof. Each D_i is finite by declaration. The product of finitely many finite sets is finite. □

Design constraint: the number of sensors n is finite and declared. No dynamic sensor discovery. This is what makes s_world finite at design time.

3.4 Fault Handling

Sensor faults are first-class values in the domain, not exceptions. When a sensor fails:

· Its value transitions to fault_value.
· The state remains total.
· Downstream logic treats the fault as a value, not an error.

This preserves determinism: the same fault always produces the same state.

---

4. SENSOR INTERFACE

4.1 The Input Alphabet Σ

Definition 3 (Input Alphabet). Σ is the set of all distinguishable transitions the agent can observe:

```
Σ = { σ : σ is a valid sensor transition }
```

Since each sensor has finite domain D_i, and the transition is a change from one value to another, the total alphabet size is bounded by:

```
|Σ| ≤ ∏_i |D_i|²
```

This is finite but astronomical. In practice, the alphabet is restricted by:

· Rate limits — only transitions within the sampling window count.
· Relevance filters — only transitions relevant to active tiles are admitted.
· Hysteresis — small changes below a declared threshold are ignored.

These filters are declared per deployment, not per standard.

4.2 The Sampling Discipline

Rule: sensors are sampled at declared rates. Sampling is synchronous with the FSM cycle. No asynchronous interrupts admit state changes.

This is a departure from typical real-time design, and it is deliberate. Asynchronous inputs violate the determinism requirement of the FSM. By sampling synchronously, the state transition is always s' = δ(s, σ) where σ is the sampled input for that cycle. No race conditions. No nondeterministic interleaving.

4.3 The Transition Function

Definition 4 (Transition). δ : S × Σ → S. Given a state and a sampled input, the next state is uniquely determined.

```
s_{t+1} = δ(s_t, σ_t)
```

Properties:

· Deterministic — δ is a function, not a relation.
· Total — δ is defined for every (s, σ) ∈ S × Σ.
· Bounded — every transition completes within the FSM cycle budget.

These three properties are what the Verifiable Agency Theorem requires (Definition 5 in the proof).

---

5. STATE ACCESS PROTOCOL

5.1 Read Access

Tiles read state through a read-only projection. Each tile declares which fields it needs:

```
Tile_Read_Scope(tile_id) ⊆ {fields of S}
```

A tile cannot read fields outside its scope. This is the isolation property (A6) applied to state.

5.2 Write Access

Writes go through declared transitions. A tile cannot arbitrarily mutate state. Instead, it proposes a transition:

```
propose_transition : S × Tile → (S → S)
```

The VERIFY state (A5) checks the proposal against declared preconditions and envelope bounds. Only after PASS does the transition commit.

Rule: no tile writes state directly. All writes are transitions mediated by VERIFY.

5.3 The State Log

Every committed transition is recorded in the append-only log (A7):

```
Entry = ⟨ timestamp, prev_state_hash, transition_id, next_state_hash, signature ⟩
```

The log is the agent's complete history. It is auditable, reproducible, and signed.

5.4 Context Hashing (for A6)

For tile invocation, the invoker provides a context hash:

```
ctx_hash = H(s_world_projection, s_ctx, task_id)
```

where H is a deterministic hash function (e.g., SHA-256 or a ternary-compatible hash). The invoked tile recomputes the hash and verifies it matches. This is the invocation integrity check from A6.

---

6. INVARIANTS

6.1 Definition

Definition 5 (Invariant Set). I ⊆ S is the set of states the agent may occupy. Every state transition must preserve I:

```
∀ s ∈ I, σ ∈ Σ : δ(s, σ) ∈ I
```

6.2 The Invariant Catalog

Each deployment declares its invariant set. Examples:

Invariant Domain Meaning
I_safe Automotive Brake pressure never exceeds safe bounds
I_bounded All Cycle counter never exceeds T_max
I_no_overflow All All integer fields stay in declared ranges
I_envelope All No action is committed outside its envelope

Rule: invariants are declared in ROM and checked by VERIFY. A transition that violates an invariant is refused with 0x-UNSAFE-ACT.

6.3 Invariant Verification

The invariant set I is finite (subset of finite S). Therefore I can be exhaustively verified by finite model-checking. This is Corollary 2 of the Verifiable Agency Theorem, applied to state.

---

7. STATE AND THE THREE FIXES

Fix How A4 Contributes
Fix 1 (Architecture) A4 fixes the shape of state. Two engineers following A4 produce structurally identical state models.
Fix 2 (Behavior) A4 leaves the content of D_i, invariants, and sensor schemas to deployment. Different domains, same grammar.
Fix 3 (Safety) A4 makes state finite and deterministic. Without this, the Verifiable Agency Theorem cannot be established. A4 is a necessary condition for Fix 3.

---

8. WHAT A4 DOES NOT SPECIFY

Honest labeling.

Item Status
