EXTENDS Naturals, FiniteSets, Sequences

CONSTANTS
    M,          \* FSM state set (finite)
    T,          \* Tile identifier set (finite)
    TaskID,     \* Task identifier space (finite)
    Sensors,    \* Sensor schema set (finite)
    Domain,     \* Sensor → finite domain
    W,          \* SRAM window size (bounded)
    MaxCycle,   \* Maximum cycle count
    Inv         \* Invariant set ⊆ S

State == [
    mode  : M,
    ctx   : T × TaskID,
    world : [s \in Sensors |-> Domain[s]],
    mem   : Seq(W),
    meta  : [cycle : 0..MaxCycle, task : TaskID]
]

Sigma == \* Valid input alphabet (bounded by sensor transitions)
    { sigma \in [Sensors -> UNION_{s \in Sensors} Domain[s]] :
        \A s \in Sensors : sigma[s] \in Domain[s] }

delta == \* Deterministic transition (deployment-supplied)
    CHOOSE f \in [State × Sigma -> State] : TRUE

Invariant_Holds(s) == s \in Inv

Preservation ==
    \A s \in Inv, sigma \in Sigma :
        Invariant_Holds(delta[s, sigma])

=============================================================================
```

The theorem Preservation is what needs to be model-checked: every transition from an invariant state leads to an invariant state.

---

10. CONCLUSION

A4 defines the state representation and world interface that the agentic extension requires. Without it, the Verifiable Agency Theorem has no premises, VERIFY has no object, and the append-only log has no content.

The three commitments:

1. State is finite. By construction, not by hope.
2. State is deterministic. Transitions are functions, not relations.
3. State is total. Faults are values, not absences.

These commitments are what make the rest of the agentic extension possible. A3 (plan representation) sits on top of A4. A5 (VERIFY) checks predicates over A4 state. A7 (memory) logs A4 transitions.

A4 is the foundation. Everything else is downstream.

