ST-T1024-A3: PLAN REPRESENTATION

The Second Load-Bearing Premise

---

Version: 1.0 (Draft)
Date: October 2026
Author: M. Taalabi (via ST-T1024 LLM-DIPLOMAT)
License: Apache 2.0
Parent Spec: ST-T1024-A0 (Agentic Extension Overview)
Dependencies: A4 (state & world model), A2 (action library)
Status: Specification. Not yet implemented. Not yet verified.
Purpose: Define the plan schema that DECOMPOSE produces and EXECUTE consumes.

---

ABSTRACT

A0 specifies that an agentic ST-T1024 decomposes goals into sub-tasks via a ROM-burned plan library. A0 does not specify what a plan is. This document specifies it.

Without A3, DECOMPOSE has no target. The plan library has no schema. The bounded-plan assumption of the Verifiable Agency Theorem (|p| ≤ K) has no grounding. A3 is the second load-bearing premise of the agentic architecture, complementing A4.

This document defines:

1. The plan schema — what a plan is, structurally.
2. The plan library — how plans are stored, indexed, and versioned.
3. The composition rules — how plans compose without unbounded recursion.
4. The bounded length property — why every plan has a finite maximum length.
5. The DECOMPOSE semantics — how a plan becomes a sub-task sequence.
6. The failure and rollback semantics — what happens when a sub-task fails.

Every definition is anchored to a requirement of the Verifiable Agency Theorem. Every constraint is a design decision.

Status: Specification. No reference implementation. No hardware validation.

---

1. INTRODUCTION: WHY PLANS COME SECOND

1.1 The Dependency Chain

```
State (A4) + Action Library (A2)
  ↓
Plans (A3)
  ├── DECOMPOSE (A1)       — plans become sub-task sequences
  ├── Composition (A8)     — plans compose via declared rules
  ├── Bounded length       — supports the Verifiable Agency Theorem
  └── Log entries (A7)     — plan execution is logged
```

A4 gives the agent a state. A2 gives the agent actions. A3 gives the agent sequences of actions — plans — that achieve goals. Without A3, the agent is a collection of isolated actions with no way to combine them into meaningful tasks.

1.2 The Two Constraints

The Verifiable Agency Theorem requires:

· Finiteness — |P| < ∞ (bounded plan library).
· Bounded length — ∀p ∈ P: |p| ≤ K (bounded plan length).

A3 must deliver both. Not by hoping plans are short, but by constructing a representation that cannot exceed K.

1.3 The Design Principle

A plan is a finite, deterministic, acyclic sequence of sub-tasks — where each sub-task is either an action from A2, a nested plan from P, or a bounded conditional on state from A4.

Plans do not invent. They compose what already exists. The plan library is the agent's procedural ROM.

---

2. PLAN SCHEMA

2.1 Definition

Definition 1 (Plan). A plan p ∈ P is a tuple:

```
p = ⟨ id, precondition, steps, postcondition, budget, version ⟩
```

where:

Field Type Description
id PlanID Unique identifier (ROM-burned)
precondition Predicate over S (A4) Required starting state
steps Sequence of Step Bounded sequence of sub-tasks
postcondition Predicate over S (A4) Expected ending state
budget {cycles, depth, energy} Declared resource bounds
version SemVer Plan version (for SRAM updates)

2.2 Step Schema

Definition 2 (Step). A step is one of three forms:

```
Step ::=
  | ActionStep ⟨action_id ∈ A, params⟩
  | PlanStep ⟨plan_id ∈ P, params⟩
  | ConditionalStep ⟨predicate over S, then_branch, else_branch⟩
```

Constraint: ConditionalStep branches must both be finite step sequences. Nesting depth is bounded by D_cond.

Constraint: PlanStep invokes a plan by ID. It does not inline the sub-plan. This keeps plans small and prevents unbounded expansion.

2.3 Bounded Length

Definition 3 (Plan Length). The length |p| is the total number of ActionStep and ConditionalStep nodes in the plan, after expanding PlanStep references to their declared depth.

Bounded Length Property (B3). Every plan has |p| ≤ K for a declared K.

Proof. K is a declared standard constant (see §4). The plan schema enforces K at:

· ROM burn time (plans longer than K are rejected).
· DECOMPOSE time (expansion that would exceed K triggers 0x-BUDGET-EXCEEDED).

The bound is enforced structurally, not empirically. □

---

3. PLAN LIBRARY

3.1 Definition

Definition 4 (Plan Library). P is the ROM-burned set of plans:

```
P = { p_1, p_2, …, p_m }
```

with m finite.

3.2 Properties

Property Meaning
Finite \|P\| = m < ∞ (Theorem assumption A3)
Indexed Plans are indexed by (goal_class, precondition_hash) for deterministic lookup
Immutable Plan bodies are ROM-burned. Only version pointers can be updated (SRAM).
Versioned Each plan has a version. Multiple versions may coexist; the pointer selects which is active.
Signed Every plan carries an SK-1024 signature over its content.

3.3 Plan Lookup

Given a goal g (from GOAL_PARSE) and current state s:

```
candidates = { p ∈ P : preconditions(p) ⊆ s  ∧  goal_class(p) = g }
```

If candidates is empty: 0x-AMBIG-GOAL.
If |candidates| > 1: deterministic tie-break (see §3.4).
If |candidates| = 1: select.

3.4 Deterministic Tie-Break

When multiple plans match, selection is by:

1. Highest version — most recently signed.
2. Lowest plan ID — lexicographic.
3. Lowest total budget — most resource-efficient.

This ordering is total and deterministic. Same input → same plan selection. Always.

---

4. STANDARD CONSTANTS

The following are declared by A3 and used by the Verifiable Agency Theorem:

Constant Meaning Typical value
K Max plan length (steps) 64
D_cond Max conditional nesting depth 4
D_invoke Max PlanStep invocation depth 3
m Max plan library size 4096
budget_max Max task-level cycle budget 10⁶

These are standard constants — deployments may tighten them, not loosen them. Loosening violates the Verifiable Agency Theorem's assumptions.

---

5. COMPOSITION RULES

5.1 The Three Composition Operators

Plans compose via three declared operators, all bounded:

Sequential composition (;). p1 ; p2 — execute p1, then p2. Both plans must be in P. Length adds: |p1 ; p2| = |p1| + |p2|. Bound: sum ≤ K.

Conditional composition (if). if predicate then p1 else p2. Both branches must be in P. Length: max(|p1|, |p2|) + 1. Bound: max + 1 ≤ K.

Bounded iteration (while_bounded). while_bounded predicate max_n p. Iteration count is bounded by declared max_n. Length: max_n × |p| + 1. Bound: product ≤ K.

Rule: composition is compile-time. Plans are composed when burned into ROM, not at runtime. Runtime composition is forbidden, because it would violate bounded length.

5.2 No Unbounded Recursion

Rule: the PlanStep invocation graph must be a DAG. Recursive plans are rejected at ROM burn time.

Proof of feasibility. DAG property is checkable at compile time by topological sort. Plans failing the check are not admitted to P. □

5.3 Total Bounded Length

Proposition. Every composed plan has |p| ≤ K.

Proof. By induction over the composition operators. Base: atomic actions have length 1 ≤ K. Sequential: sum of bounded terms, bounded by K by declared constraint. Conditional: max of bounded terms plus 1, bounded by K. Iteration: product of bounded term and max_n, bounded by K. DAG property prevents infinite expansion. □

---

6. DECOMPOSE SEMANTICS

6.1 Definition

Definition 5 (DECOMPOSE). Given a selected plan p, DECOMPOSE produces a sub-task sequence:

```
DECOMPOSE(p, s) : (Plan, State) → Sequence(SubTask)
```

The sequence is finite, ordered, and deterministic.

6.2 The Decomposition Rules

Plan Node Decomposition
ActionStep(a, params) One sub-task: invoke action a with params
PlanStep(p', params) Recursive DECOMPOSE, up to D_invoke depth
ConditionalStep(pred, t, e) Evaluate pred on s; decompose selected branch

The sequence is built depth-first, in declared order.

6.3 Sub-Task Ordering

Sub-tasks execute in sequence. Each sub-task:

1. Reads its declared state projection (A4 §5.1).
2. Executes (via A1 FSM).
3. Verifies (via A5).
4. Commits or refuses.
5. Logs (via A7).

If a sub-task refuses: see §7.

6.4 Bounded Decomposition

Proposition. |DECOMPOSE(p, s)| ≤ K × D_invoke.

Proof. Each ActionStep produces one sub-task. Each PlanStep recursively expands, bounded by D_invoke. Total sub-tasks bounded by plan length × invocation depth. □

---

7. FAILURE AND ROLLBACK SEMANTICS

7.1 Failure Modes

Failure Cause Response
Sub-task refused VERIFY = FAIL Apply plan's declared failure policy
Sub-task timeout T-AET exceeded 0x-BUDGET-EXCEEDED
Invariant violated State left I 0x-UNSAFE-ACT, halt
Plan preconditions unmet State drifted 0x-AMBIG-GOAL, refuse plan

7.2 Failure Policy

Each plan declares a failure policy in its schema:

```
FailurePolicy ::=
  | Halt              \* Stop, log, refuse
  | Retry(n)          \* Retry sub-task up to n times
  | RollbackTo(step)  \* Return to step k, retry from there
  | Skip              \* Continue to next sub-task (dangerous, discouraged)
```

Rule: default policy is Halt. Deployments may override, but every override is declared and auditable.

Rule: retry count is bounded. The total cycles consumed by a plan — including retries — must not exceed budget_max.

7.3 Rollback

Rollback restores state to a prior checkpoint. Checkpoints are recorded in the append-only log (A7). Rollback itself is an action with its own envelope; it must pass VERIFY like any other action.

Rule: rollback cannot violate invariants. If the state before the failure was in I, and rollback returns to that state, I is preserved.

---

8. PLAN AND THE THREE FIXES

Fix How A3 Contributes
Fix 1 (Architecture) A3 fixes the shape of plans. Every conforming implementation has the same plan schema, same composition rules, same DECOMPOSE semantics.
Fix 2 (Behavior) A3 leaves the content of P to deployment. Automotive plans ≠ grid plans. Same grammar, different libraries.
Fix 3 (Safety) A3 delivers the bounded-length property. Without it, DECOMPOSE could produce unbounded sub-task sequences, violating the Verifiable Agency Theorem.

