10. TLA+ SKELETON

```tla
--------------------------- MODULE STT1024_A3_Plans --------------------------
EXTENDS Naturals, FiniteSets, Sequences

CONSTANTS
    A,          \* Action library (A2, finite)
    P,          \* Plan library (finite)
    K,          \* Max plan length
    D_invoke,   \* Max invocation depth
    S           \* State space (A4)

Step ==
    [ type   : {"action", "plan", "conditional"},
      target : A ∪ P,
      pred   : S -> BOOLEAN,
      branches : Seq(Seq(Step)) ]

Plan ==
    [ id           : STRING,
      precondition : S -> BOOLEAN,
      steps        : Seq(Step),
      postcondition: S -> BOOLEAN,
      budget       : [cycles : Nat, depth : Nat] ]

\* Bounded length property
BoundedPlan(p) ==
    /\ p \in Plan
    /\ Len(p.steps) <= K
    /\ \A i \in DOMAIN p.steps : StepBounded(p.steps[i], 0)

StepBounded(step, depth) ==
    /\ depth <= D_invoke
    /\ CASE step.type = "action"      -> TRUE
            [] step.type = "plan"      -> depth < D_invoke
            [] step.type = "conditional" -> 
                 \A b \in DOMAIN step.branches :
                     \A i \in DOMAIN step.branches[b] :
                         StepBounded(step.branches[b][i], depth + 1)

\* The theorem A3 contributes to
Theorem_A3 == \A p \in P : BoundedPlan(p)

=============================================================================
```

The theorem Theorem_A3 is what must be established: every plan in P satisfies the bounded-length property. This can be checked at ROM burn time (compile-time verification) and does not require runtime model-checking.

---

11. CONCLUSION

A3 defines the plan representation that DECOMPOSE produces and EXECUTE consumes. Combined with A4, the two load-bearing premises of the Verifiable Agency Theorem are now in place:

· A4 — finite state and world model.
· A3 — finite plan library with bounded plans.

With these, the Verifiable Agency Theorem's assumptions (A2, A3, A4 in the proof) are grounded. The theorem becomes dischargeable.

The three commitments:

1. Plans are finite. By construction, not by hope.
2. Plans are bounded. Every plan has |p| ≤ K.
3. Plans are compositional but not recursive. DAG-only, compile-time composition.

These commitments, together with A4, make the agentic architecture complete at the specification level.

---

APPENDIX A — Plain Language

What is a plan?
A recipe. A sequence of steps to get from here to there.
Who writes the recipes?
The people who deploy the agent. They burn them into ROM.
Can the agent invent new recipes?
No. It can only select from the ones that were burned in.
Why is that good?
Because it means the agent cannot do anything you did not already approve.
What if no recipe fits?
The agent refuses with 0x-AMBIG-GOAL. It doesn't improvise.
What if a step fails?
The plan declares a failure policy. Halt, retry, rollback. All bounded. All logged.
What's the guarantee?
Every plan has a maximum length. Every sub-task is bounded. The agent cannot accidentally run forever.
What is this called?
A3 — the plan representation.

